 "sources": [
  {
   "url": "https://www.boeing.com/content/dam/boeing/v2/airports/acaps/DC9.pdf",
   "title": "Boeing (Douglas Aircraft Company), DC-9 Airplane Characteristics for Airport Planning (June 1984, revised F 5/84), 2.2 General Airplane Dimensions: Model DC-9-15 and Model DC-9-32; 2.7.4 Passenger Entrance and Service Door Clearances, Model DC-9; 2.3 Ground Clearances, Model DC-9-15; 7.2 Footprint, Model DC-9; 2.2 plan's flap and aileron lines; 3.4 FAR Landing Runway Length Requirements, Model DC-9-15 (flaps 50)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-24",
   "note": "Dimensions and outlines read off the DC-9-15 sheet's side, plan and front views (tools/trace/dc91/), starting from the DC-9-30's points (tools/trace/dc93/) with its plugs taken out; the figures are the aircraft's measurements, not a copy of the drawing. The sheet is a 1-bit scan on graph paper (about 47 px a meter, so outlines are good to about 5 cm), drawn half a degree off level (turned level). Scaled by the printed 28.07 m from the nose to the tail cone. The wing is this sheet's own (27.25 m span, no leading-edge slats); fin, tailplane, JT8D nacelle and nose are the DC-9-30's, snapped onto this sheet's drawing of them. Its side and front views draw the tube 3.50 m and 3.46 m tall against the printed 3.61 m (the DC-9-30's sheet draws the same section within 0.6%), so the fuselage is fit to the printed height (fit_height.py). The sheet draws 25 cabin windows (a dashed one at each end, optional, left out), the main entry door, one overwing exit a side and the cargo doors (dashed); the service door is placed from 2.7.4 as on the DC-9-30. Windows' height and width and the cockpit panes are the MD-80's (MD82: the same nose and cabin section). Confidence medium: a scan. Its plan draws the wing 3.1% short of the printed 27.25 m span (26.41 m; the front view draws 27.18 m): the shape keeps the plan's wing, the one drawing of its planform. Gear (`gear`): 2.2 prints the nose gear 2.32 m (7 ft 7.2 in) behind the nose and the wheelbase 13.32 m (43 ft 8.4 in); 7.2 prints the track 4.98 m (16 ft 4 in) (2.2 prints 5.00 m, used here), the main wheels 24 in (0.61 m) apart and the nose wheels 14 in (0.36 m), the main tires 40 x 14-16 and the nose tires 26 x 6.6. The ground is -2.79 m: the crown less M (4.60 m) gives -2.78, the fin-top fairing less F (8.38 m) -2.80, the nacelle's middle less J (2.96 m) -2.86, the wingtip less G (2.24 m) -2.73 (the middles of 2.3's maximum and minimum). Flap hinge fairings (`wing.fairings`): the DC-9-32 sheet's front view (2.2, and 2.3's) draws four under each wing, at 4.09, 4.59, 6.45 and 8.52 m out, 0.27-0.41 m deep, and its side view draws the near wing's as short V-shaped pods at the trailing edge (0.98, 0.66 and 0.38 m long; the 4.59 m one is hidden behind the first and given its length). The side view draws them ending 0.2-0.5 m ahead of the plan's trailing edge, so each is moved aft to end 0.16 m behind it (as the other shapes' fairings drawn ahead of it). The DC-9-15 sheet's front view draws the same fairings; they're the DC-9-32's, each kept at its distance from the trailing edge. Flaps: the DC-9-32's span and chord (2.2's plans), no slats (the DC-9-15 sheet's leading edge is one line), landing setting off 3.4's chart."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19960003437",
   "title": "Yamamoto, Donelson, Huang and Joshi, Airframe Noise Prediction Evaluation, NASA Contractor Report 4695 (October 1995): Figure 1.1, Outboard Flap Section (a) DC-9-31, (b) DC-10-10; section 5 (\"DC-9-30 series aircraft have one double-slotted flap\"; \"DC-10-10 has two flaps\")",
   "accessed": "2026-11-26",
   "note": "The flaps' slots: a vane ahead of the flap on the DC-9-31's and the DC-10-10's outboard flap (the DC-10's inboard flap isn't drawn there and is taken to be the same)."
  }
